  "findings": [],
  "summary": "Audited 52 clients over 20 rounds of FedAvg logistic regression, spending 19.5 of 20 epsilon on all-client screens (client_updates, client_performance, drift_by_round, feature_summary, subgroup_outcomes for Sex, AgeGroup and Income) and targeted follow-ups (GU updates and drift, WA updates). No client crossed the |z|>=6 bar, and no fault showed up consistently across two independent tools. Leads, all at noise-adjusted |z| of about 3-4 and mostly from small clients, were not reported: GU (n_train 708) has a size-adjusted update-norm z of +3.9, a CITL z of -3.9 on final-round performance and the lowest mean update cosine, but its per-round update and drift values are very noisy at epsilon 1 and its AUC is normal; WA (n_train 11181) has consistently about 3x median update norm over rounds 1-10, falling to about 1.7-2x later, with positive cosine and normal performance, which fits its large size (size-adjusted z +3.6); DC has a drift z of +3.9 (DiffWalk) on a small subgroup; SC has an FNR z of -3.7 for Sex=0 with normal O/E. Subgroup O/E outliers (WI Income 7, DC AgeGroup 1, CA AgeGroup 8) come from tiny expected counts and show observed rates above predicted, not the under-diagnosis pattern (high FNR). The federation looks clean. GU is the one client worth re-checking if more budget becomes available, since its CITL and update-norm leads point the same way across two tools."
